EXT. ROWBOAT - MIDDLE OF A MIST-BOUND LAKE - DAWN

A battered rowboat drifts on glass-flat water, fog pressing in from every side. The oars lie shipped. A tackle box rests open between two seats.

BEATRIX LOWELL, sixties, in a yellow slicker, holds a rod. Her daughter CAROLINE LOWELL, thirties, sits opposite, hands wrapped round a thermos. Neither has caught a thing. Neither has spoken for an hour. A loon calls across the water. Beatrix reels in. Caroline watches the line.

BEATRIX
You never liked fishing.

CAROLINE
I liked sitting with you.

BEATRIX
That's not the same.

CAROLINE
It was for me.

Beatrix casts again. The lure arcs, plops. She does not look up.

BEATRIX
She called me, you know.

CAROLINE
Who?

BEATRIX
Your sister. She said you'd been dodging her.

CAROLINE
She's the one who stopped answering.

BEATRIX
That's not what she said.

CAROLINE
(rising from the seat)
Mother, please.

BEATRIX ^
Sit down before you tip us!

CAROLINE
You always do this!

She stands. The boat rocks. Beatrix grabs the gunwale. She lifts her eyes, finally, and the two women glare at each other across the fog, the loon crying again, closer.
